<Base title="How Long Does a Furnace Last? Signs It's Time to Replace" description="Gas furnaces typically last 15 to 20 years. How to find your furnace's age, the signs it's near the end, and when replacing beats repairing.">
  <section>
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › Guides › Furnace lifespan</p>
    <h1>How long does a furnace last?</h1>
    <p class="lede">A gas furnace typically lasts <b>15 to 20 years</b>. Electric furnaces often last a little longer because they have fewer parts that wear out. The first step is to find out how old yours is: <a href="/furnace-age/">decode the serial number</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Signs a furnace is near the end</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It is over 15 years old and needing repairs more than once a season.</li>
      <li>Heating bills rising without a change in use.</li>
      <li>Uneven heat, short cycling, or the blower running much longer than it used to.</li>
      <li>A yellow, flickering burner flame instead of a steady blue one, soot, or a carbon monoxide alarm. Turn it off and call a technician.</li>
      <li>Rust or cracks on the heat exchanger, found during a service visit.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Repair or replace?</h2>
    <p>A common rule of thumb: multiply the furnace's age by the repair cost. If the result is over about $5,000, or the repair costs more than a third of a new furnace, replacing it usually makes more sense. Furnaces made before 1992 were built before the federal 78% efficiency minimum, so replacing one with a modern 95% furnace also cuts fuel use.</p>
  </section>
  <p class="note">A furnace's age is also useful when buying or selling a home: inspectors note it in their report, and buyers use it to plan for a replacement.</p>
  <p class="note">How old are other people's furnaces? See our <a href="/statistics/furnace-age/">furnace age statistics</a>, by state and region.</p>
</Base>
